; NextDAAD Layer 2 TRANSPARENCY + hardware fixture (2026-08-07, rev 1).
;
; Compiled and staged by tests\build-tests.ps1 -L2Holes, which also
; generates the punch-out card with tests\art\mkl2holes.py (001.NXI,
; 256x192) and copies build\nextdaad.nex in. sd\L2HOLES\ is emptied and
; refilled on every run and holds nothing but GAME.DDB, 001.NXI and
; NEXTDAAD.NEX - copy the folder to the card and launch NEXTDAAD.NEX
; from inside it.
;
; RUN SHEET: docs\superpowers\l2-holes-run-sheet.md. Read it before
; running; it names every hole and says what a failure of each one means.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT THIS FIXTURE PUTS ON SCREEN, AND WHY EACH PIECE IS THERE
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE TEXT, FIRST. Window 0 is the WHOLE tilemap - 80 columns by 32 rows,
; every cell written, paper 1 (dark blue). The text is a position ruler:
; the screen is divided into ten 8-column UNITS per row, and unit u of
; row r reads
;
;     rr - cc t t .        e.g.  "12-16DG."  row 12, column 16, tag DG
;
; so ANY 8 columns of ANY row states its own row number, its own column
; number and the name of whatever is meant to be showing there. That is
; the entire point: Layer 2 covers this text, the card punches holes in
; Layer 2, and what you read through a hole tells you which hole it is
; without counting cells on the glass.
;
; The eighth character of every unit is a spacer, and every SECOND unit
; ends with a real space rather than a dot. Those spaces are structural,
; not decoration: NextDAAD buffers printable non-space characters into
; wrapBuf and only emits them when a space arrives or the buffer reaches
; the window width (src\print.asm, prn_char / prn_flush). Each MES here
; is exactly 15 non-space characters followed by one space, so it flushes
; at a known column with the INK that was set immediately before it.
; Without that space the characters would sit in the buffer and be
; emitted later under a LATER ink, and the whole colour layout would
; collapse onto one colour per row. Do not "tidy" the trailing spaces
; away, and do not merge two MES lines into one.
;
; PER-CHARACTER COLOUR. Five INK changes per row, one per 16-column
; segment, stepping through six inks (15 14 13 7 6 10 - bright white,
; bright yellow, bright cyan, white, yellow, bright red) with the phase
; advanced by the row number, so the colours run in diagonal bands. Per
; character colour is a tilemap feature this build owns (each cell holds
; its own paper/ink pair, src\tilemap.asm) and a fixture that fills the
; whole tilemap should exercise it. GREEN IS DELIBERATELY NOT IN THE INK
; SET - see "the green signature" below.
;
; THE LAST ROW IS ONE CELL SHORT, BY DESIGN. win_newline scrolls the
; window when the cursor advances past the last row (src\windows.asm),
; and printing an 80th character on row 31 would wrap, newline, and
; scroll the whole screen up by one - destroying row 0. Row 31 therefore
; stops at column 78 and cell (31,79) is left as the CLS blank. It is in
; the control margin and carries nothing.
;
; THE CARD. PICTURE 1 + DISPLAY 0 loads 001.NXI - a 256x192 generated
; card (tests\art\mkl2holes.py) that is FULL SCREEN for a 256-wide
; picture: gfx_derive_height caps mode 0 at 192 rows. It covers tilemap
; ROWS 4..27 and COLUMNS 8..71 exactly, because Layer 2 in 256x192 mode
; is inset 32 pixels from the tilemap origin on both axes (dev guide
; chapter-next-tilemap.tex:18 "Tilemap layer overlaps ULA by 32 pixels on
; each side", chapter-next-layer2.tex:308), so L2 row r lands on tilemap
; row r/8 + 4 and L2 column c on tilemap column c/4 + 8.
;
; PICTURE is a CONDITION, so a failed load aborts its entry and the next
; one clears the screen and says so, instead of the fixture silently
; testing nothing - which is exactly how the 2026-08-03 sfxdi run failed.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE CONTROL MARGIN - tagged CM, and it must ALWAYS be readable
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The card cannot reach tilemap columns 0..7 or 72..79, nor rows 0..3 or
; 28..31. Every unit in that margin is tagged CM and it is a CONTROL: it
; is outside Layer 2's footprint entirely, so it must stay legible with
; the card up, with the card cleared, and in every state in between.
;
; CM text that is covered, blanked or corrupted is NOT a transparency
; result. It means Layer 2 is being displayed in the wrong mode or at the
; wrong origin (a 320x256 surface WOULD cover the margin), or that the
; tilemap itself has been damaged. Nothing below can be read as an answer
; about transparency until the CM ring is intact.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE HOLES, AND WHAT EACH ONE ANSWERS
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Thirteen holes at index 255 (L2_TRANSP_INDEX, src\nextdaad.inc:266 -
; the one value l2_pal9_stamp makes transparent after any picture load).
; Each is placed so the text under it names it:
;
;   TL TR BL BR   32x16 corner holes on the extreme corner pixels - the
;                 first and last bytes of the first and last rows. Each
;                 shows two rows of ONE whole text unit: "04-08TL" and
;                 "05-08TL" top left, "04-64TR" top right, "26-08BL" and
;                 "26-64BR" at the bottom. A corner that shows white
;                 frame instead of text is a clipped or short blit.
;   DG            a 48x48 right triangle with a one-pixel-per-row
;                 diagonal edge, tapering to a SINGLE PIXEL at the top.
;                 Tagged DG on unit 2, readable at rows 15..17. A stepped
;                 or squared-off edge means transparency is being decided
;                 per byte-run rather than per pixel.
;   CT            the centre hole, a radius-24 disc at the middle of the
;                 screen. Tagged CT on units 4 and 5, rows 13..19; the
;                 widest row (16) shows both.
;   RN            a ring, outer radius 23, inner radius 6, with an OPAQUE
;                 ISLAND in the middle. Tagged RN on units 6 and 7. The
;                 island is the test: if it disappears, transparency is
;                 being applied by area instead of by pixel value.
;   32 16 08      the size ladder: 32x32, 16x16 and 8x8 square holes,
;                 tagged with their own pixel size on units 2, 4 and 6 of
;                 rows 20..23.
;   4C            a 4x8 hole - EXACTLY ONE TILEMAP CELL, the smallest
;                 hole that can still show a readable character. It sits
;                 on the tag of unit 3 row 21 and shows the single
;                 character "4".
;   (2x2, 1x1)    two sub-cell holes with no readable glyph: a 2x2 pixel
;                 hole at row 21 column 46 and a ONE PIXEL hole at row 21
;                 column 59. Both sit at the top-left corner of their
;                 cell, which is blank in every glyph, so each shows a
;                 dot of the DARK BLUE PAPER. Each sits at the centre of
;                 a sand-coloured target patch with a red crosshair,
;                 because a one-byte hole in a 49,152-byte picture cannot
;                 be found by scanning. The 1x1 hole is the single most
;                 sensitive element in this fixture.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE DODGE TEST - two blocks that must BOTH stay opaque
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Tilemap rows 9..11 carry two adjacent magenta blocks, labelled E3 and
; E7 in black. The left one uses a palette entry whose RRRGGGBB byte
; packs to $E3 = L2_TRANSP_COLOUR; the right one uses the safe escape
; (7,1,7), which packs to $E7. l2_pal9_run (src\overlay2.asm)
; rewrites any $E3 entry to $E7 before programming it, so BOTH blocks
; must render as magenta and INDISTINGUISHABLE - the dodged E3 entry
; lands exactly on the E7 control colour - and NEITHER may show text.
;
; If the E3 block shows text, the dodge failed, and any artist whose
; palette happens to contain saturated magenta gets holes punched through
; their own picture. The text under those blocks is tagged so the verdict
; is not a matter of remembering which side is which: units 3 and 4 of
; rows 9..11 read E3, units 5 and 6 read E7.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE GREEN SIGNATURE - a colour that must never appear
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Palette entry 255 in the card FILE is bright green (0,7,0). It should
; never reach the screen: l2_pal9_stamp overwrites entry 255 with $E3
; after the copy loop on every path. Green is used nowhere else on the
; card and is excluded from this fixture's ink set, so if the holes come
; up bright green instead of showing text, the stamp did not run. That is
; a different fault from the holes not being punched at all, and it now
; has its own signature instead of both looking like "wrong colour".
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; DARK BLUE IS THE BINARY DETECTOR
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Paper 1 = dadPalette[1] = RGB333 (0,0,6) (src\tilemap.asm:247). The
; card carries no blue at all apart from white and the two magentas, and
; no large flat blue area anywhere. So blue INSIDE the card footprint is
; a hole and nothing else - which is what makes the 1x1 and 2x2 holes
; judgeable at all, since no glyph is readable at that size and the
; colour is the entire readout.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE A/B TOGGLE - the only interaction
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; There is no parser. A parser prompt would print into the full-screen
; window and scroll the ruler away. Every keypress calls PROCESS 4, which
; alternates DISPLAY 1 (clear the Layer 2 plane to transparent - the
; WHOLE ruler is then visible and is the reference reading) and DISPLAY 0
; (blit the card again). Comparing the two states IS the test: with the
; card cleared you read what SHOULD be under each hole, with the card up
; you read what actually is.
;
; SYSMESS 16 IS A SINGLE SPACE, AND THE KEY WAIT HAPPENS IN A 1x1 WINDOW.
; h_anykey prints SM16 before it blocks (src\overlay0.asm:1858). Printed
; into the full-screen window at the end of row 31 it would wrap, newline
; and scroll the entire screen. Window 1 is therefore a 1x1 window parked
; on cell (31,79) - the one cell row 31 leaves blank - and is selected
; before the loop: prn_char swallows a space at column 0
; (src\print.asm:127), so ANYKEY prints nothing at all, however many
; times it runs. Do not give SM16 real text and do not run ANYKEY with
; window 0 selected.
;
; NO SOUND, NO MUSIC, NO SECOND PICTURE. Nothing is staged but the card,
; so a symptom cannot be an audio interaction, a song's interrupt hold or
; a stale asset winning the probe chain. The probe chain matters here:
; NX2 variants are tried FIRST, so a leftover 001.NX2 would route the
; blit through gfx_row_scatter320 and change the geometry under the test.
; sd\L2HOLES\ is emptied on every stage and holds three files.

; System flags used by name (DAAD BLANK template spellings)
#define fPlayer             38
#define fToggle             100

/CTL
_
/VOC
NORTH  2  noun
SOUTH  3  noun
LOOK   4  verb
/STX
